{
  "dom_id": "step-q109670",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(2,3)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(7,25)$ are independent and $Y=5{X}_{1}-{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y>20).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ | $\\,0.9370\\,$ | $\\,0.9382\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9452\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ | $\\,0.9484\\,$ | $\\,0.9495\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9554\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ | $\\,0.9591\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=5{X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N(5{\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{5}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5\\cdot 2-7,25\\cdot 3+25) \\\\  & ∼N(3,100).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =3$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{100}=10.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y>20).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>20) & =P(Z>\\frac{20-3}{10}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.7) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.7) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.7)≈0.9554.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>20) & =1-Φ(1.7) \\\\  & =1-0.9554 \\\\  & ≈0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
